{{current}}

'''Legislative elections''' were held in [[Australia]] on [[9 October]], [[2004]]. The conservative [[Coalition (Australia)|Coalition]] of the [[Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal Party]], led by [[Prime Minister of Australia|Prime Minister]] [[John Howard]], and the [[National Party of Australia]] led by [[Deputy Prime Minister of Australia|Deputy Prime Minister]] [[John Anderson (Australian politician)|John Anderson]], won a fourth three-year term, with a net gain of about three seats in the 150-member [[Australian House of Representatives|House of Representatives]].

[[image:ac.pollingbooth.jpg|thumb|300px|Party workers hand out How-to-Vote Cards at a polling place in [[St Kilda]], [[Melbourne]], in the Division of [[Melbourne Ports]], the day before the [[9 October]], [[2004]], federal election. One polling booth in each electorate was available for "pre-polling" the week before the elections. Placards for the [[Liberal Party of Australia]] and the [[Australian Greens]] candidates can be seen.]]

The coalition parties won 46.7 percent of the primary vote, a gain of 3.7 percent over the [[Australian legislative election, 2001|2001 election]]. [[Australian Labor Party|Labor]] polled 37.7 percent, a loss of 0.2 percent. The [[Australian Greens]] emerged as the most prominent minor party, polling 7.1 percent, a gain of 2.1 percent. Both the [[Australian Democrats]] and [[One Nation]] had their vote greatly reduced. After a notional distribution of preferences, the [[Australian Electoral Commission]] estimated that the Coalition had polled 52.7 percent of the [[Australian electoral system|two-party preferred vote]], a gain of 2.1 percent from 2001. 

The Liberal Party has won 74 seats and the National Party 12 seats, against the Australian Labor Party opposition's 58 seats. Three independent members were re-elected. Three seats are still in doubt. The Coalition also seems likely to win 39 seats in the 76-member [[Australian Senate|Senate]], making the Howard Government the first government to have a majority in the Senate since [[1981]]. 

[[image:Mackerras_2004_Federal Election.png|thumb|left|100px|[[Malcolm Mackerras|Mackerras]] Pendulum for the 2004 Federal Election (84kB)]]

The election result was a triumph for Howard, who is now set to become Australia's second-longest serving Prime Minister, and who will see the election result as a vindication of his policies, particularly his decision to join in the [[Iraq War|2003 invasion of Iraq]]. The results were a setback for the Labor leader, [[Mark Latham]], and makes Labor's task in winning [[Australian legislative election, 2007|the next election]] more difficult.

A provisional pendulum for the new House of Representatives can be seen at [http://psephos.adam-carr.net/countries/a/australia/2004/pendulum20041.txt Adam Carr's Electoral Archive]. It shows that at the next election Labor will need to win about 15 seats and gain a swing of over 4 percent.

Members and Senators defeated in the election include [[Larry Anthony]], the National Party Minister for Children and Youth Affairs, defeated in Richmond, New South Wales; former Labor minister [[Con Sciacca]], defeated in Bonner, Queensland; Liberal Parliamentary Secretaries [[Trish Worth]] (Adelaide, South Australia) and [[Ross Cameron]] (Parramatta, New South Wales); and Democrat Senators [[Aden Ridgeway]] (the only Aboriginal member of the outgoing Parliament), [[Brian Greig]] and [[John Cherry]]. Liberal Senator [[John Tierney]] (New South Wales), who was dropped to number four on the Coalition Senate ticket, was also defeated.

[[image:ac.pollingday.jpg|thumb|300px|A party worker for the [[Australian Labor Party]] hands out How-to-Vote Cards at a polling place in [[St Kilda]], [[Melbourne]], in the Division of [[Melbourne Ports]], on election day, [[9 October]], [[2004]].]]

Celebrity candidates [[Peter Garrett]] (Labor, Kingsford Smith, New South Wales) and [[Malcolm Turnbull]] (Liberal, Wentworth, New South Wales) easily won their contests. Prominent clergyman [[Fred Nile]] failed to win a Senate seat in New South Wales. The first [[Islam|Muslim]] candidate to be endorsed by a major party in Australia, [[Ed Husic]], failed to win the seat of Greenway, New South Wales, for Labor. The former [[One Nation Party|One Nation]] leader, [[Pauline Hanson]], failed in her bid to win a Senate seat in Queensland as an independent. 

Detailed results are available at the [http://vtr.aec.gov.au Australian Electoral Commission]'s virtual tally room website.

Minor parties had mixed results. The [[Australian Democrats]] polled their lowest vote since their creation in [[1977]], and will lose the three Senate seats they were defending. The [[Australian Greens]] have won one Senate seat in Western Australia, and are favoured to win another in Tasmania. They missed seats in Victoria, Queensland and South Australia, partly because of preference deals by other parties. This was a poorer result than they had expected. They failed to win a seat in the House, losing the seat of Cunningham which they gained at a [[2002]] by-election. 

The [[Australian Progressive Alliance]] leader, Senator [[Meg Lees]], and the [[One Nation Party|One Nation]] parliamentary leader, Senator [[Len Harris]], lost their seats. One Nation's vote in the House of Representatives collapsed. The [[Christian Democratic Party (Australia)|Christian Democratic Party]], the [[Citizens Electoral Council]], the [[Democratic Labor Party]], the [[Progressive Labour Party]] and the [[Socialist Alliance (Australia)|Socialist Alliance]] all failed to make any impact. The [[Family First Party]] polled 2 percent of the vote nationally, and will win a Senate seat in Victoria. Following the distribution of preferences, it is possible that they will also win a seat in Tasmania over Greens candidate [[Christine Milne]].

